the u.s president says the august 31st  deadline still stands  we are currently on a pace to finish by  august the 31st  the sooner we can finish the better  each day of operations brings added risk  to our troops  but  the completion by august 31st depends  upon the taliban continuing to cooperate  and allow access to the airport for  those who were trans were transporting  out and no disruptions to our operations  military aircraft continue to take off  from kabul airport it's a 24-hour  operation  on board some of the tens of thousands  of people who have been evacuated since  the taliban seized power on august the  15th  but it's estimated tens of thousands  more are desperate to leave  despite pressure from governments around  the world and before the u.s president  made his statement the taliban  reiterated there would be no extension  of the august 31st deadline for the  remaining nato soldiers in afghanistan  to get out  we will not allow an extension beyond  august 31st so the us should remove and  evacuate all personnel by the set  deadline  mujahid said the taliban is stopping  afghans from going to the airport  the islamic emirate is trying to  encourage people to go home but the  americans are inviting people to go to  the airport their adverse policy of  continuing to board people on planes the  airport access route has been closed to  avoid stampedes  the un's top human rights official told  an emergency session of the un human  rights council she has received credible  reports of the taliban committing  serious violations including summary  executions of civilians and restrictions  on women a fundamental red line will be  the taliban's treatment of women and  girls and respect for their rights to  liberty freedom of movement education  self-expression and employment guided by  international human rights norms in  particular ensuring access to quality  secondary education for girls will be an  essential indicators of commitment to  human rights  the taliban spokesman said women should  for the time being stay at home because  taliban fighters were not yet trained on  how to behave  we need to have a procedure and a plan  in place security force is not  operational they are not trained to deal  with women that's why we're stopping  women from going to work we ask women to  stay home and their salaries will be  resuming  come back to work when we have a system  in place  with no extension of the august 31st  deadline there are mounting concerns for  tens of thousands of afghans that want  to leave this country  the taliban says they have nothing to  fear and that there will be no reprisals  against people who have worked with nato  foreign organizations or the media in  the past  but trusting the taliban on the basis of  their words alone is something that many  afghans and the international community  find very difficult to do  charles stratford al jazeera kabul
 

